//
// AN APPEND TO A RECORD FIELD LANDS IN ITS OWN SLOT (`@FR-R-FieldAppend`).  A straight-line append to
// a record's vector field writes the element in that field's own slot, for every element shape, and so
// does a plain append beside a call that builds into a destination.  Each section keeps its own cases
// and receipt.
//
// ── a-straight-line-append-to-a-record-field-is-its-own-slot-for-every-element-shape ──
//
// A STRAIGHT-LINE APPEND TO A RECORD FIELD IS ITS OWN SLOT FOR EVERY ELEMENT SHAPE
// (`@FR-R-FieldAppend`).  `r.f += [x]` outside any loop, over a plain vector field of a struct,
// is emitted as the field's own append — the slot claimed at the field's offset with the
// element's size, prefilled with the element's defaults unless the literal writes every field,
// the length bumped at the finish — where the template dispatched per element.  The bare clause
// does the same for a `v += [x]` in a loop the header hoist declines.  Whatever the element —
// a partial struct literal (a declared default, a null, a zero, a text), a character, a
// struct-enum variant of any width, a keyed collection field that keeps the template — the
// vector must hold exactly the elements appended, in order, with every omitted field at its
// default, across the growth steps.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  the element shape (partial struct literal, integer, character,
//                        struct-enum variants of three widths, keyed collections), the count
//                        (zero, one, thirty through a recursion that appends straight-line
//                        past several growth steps), the field's siblings (three vector fields
//                        of one record), the form (field clause, bare clause with a writer
//                        call in the loop).
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the parent is a plain struct local; no push header holds the field.
// Over the existing cell it ADDS: the field clause on partial literals (its default / null /
//                        text prefill), the enum clause, the scalar and character elements,
//                        the keyed fields that must NOT take the clause, and growth.
//
// Expected values by hand.  Pt { x: n } omits y (declared default 7), tag ("") and z (an
// `integer?`, null).
//   grow(b, 30) appends n = 30 down to 1: pts x = 30, 29, ..., 1: pts[0].x = 30, pts[14].x = 16,
//     pts[29].x = 1, Σx = 465; every y = 7: Σy = 210; every tag "" and z null (count 30 each);
//     ints n * n: Σ n² for 1..30 = 30·31·61/6 = 9455, ints[0] = 900, ints[29] = 1;
//     chars 'é' for even n, 'z' for odd: 15 of each.
//   grow(b, 0) and grow(b, 1): lengths 0 and 1 (x = 1, y = 7).
//   add(s, k) appends Rect { w: k, h: k + 1, tag: 9 }, Circle { r: k }, Dot:  add(s, 1), add(s, 2)
//     give positions 0 Rect(1, 2, 9), 1 Circle(1), 2 Dot, 3 Rect(2, 3, 9), 4 Circle(2), 5 Dot;
//     Σ w·h = 2 + 6 = 8, Σ r = 3, Σ tag = 18, 2 dots, length 6.
//   keyed: a sorted<Item[id]> field appended 5, 3, 9 iterates 3, 5, 9; a hash<Entry[id]> field
//     (a separate record type: two collections of one type in a record are one linked group)
//     appended the same finds id 9's v = 90, id 3's v = 30, id 4 absent, and has 3 entries.
//   two(5): a gets Pt { x: i } for i in 0..5 (Σx 10; y 7 each = 35; tag "") and mk(i + 100) for
//     the even i = 0, 2, 4 (x 100, 102, 104 = 306; y = 2x = 200 + 204 + 208 = 612; tags t100,
//     t102, t104): 8 records, Σx = 316, Σy = 647, tags "t100t102t104".  b gets mk(i): 5
//     records, Σx 10, Σy = 2·(0+1+2+3+4) = 20, tags "t0t1t2t3t4".
//   dyn(3, v): x = 0, 1, 2 from mk's, y the default 7, tags "t0t1t2": "3:3:21:t0t1t2";
//     dyn(0, v) "0:0:0:".
//   whiler(4): x 0..3 (Σ 6), y 7 each (28), tags "t0t1t2t3"; whiler(200) breaks when the
//     vector holds 101 records: Σx = 0 + ... + 100 = 5050, Σy = 707, tags t0...t100.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a coverage cell for a rewrite already correct on main; reach shown by
//   `LOFT_REWRITE_CENSUS_FN=<file> loft --native-c`: `R-FieldAppend` 3 in `add` and 1 in `grow`
//   (the field clause), `R-FieldAppend/bare` in `two` (3) and `whiler` (1); `dyn` is a decline that must answer the same.  The C
//   writer takes the clause unconditionally; `LOFT_NO_FIELD_APPEND=1` / `LOFT_NO_BARE_APPEND=1`
//   switch it on rustc-native only (not built here).  The interpreter is the oracle.
//
// ── a-plain-append-beside-a-destination-call-lands-in-its-own-slot ──
//
// A PLAIN APPEND BESIDE A DESTINATION CALL LANDS IN ITS OWN SLOT (`@FR-R-FieldAppend` crossed
// with `@FR-R-Destination`).  R-FieldAppend's BARE clause emits a mint group over a plain
// vector that no push header holds — "in a loop whose header hoist declines, beside a
// destination call (R-Destination), outside a group" — as the vector's own append, and its
// FIELD clause does the same for a straight-line `r.f += [x]`.  R-Destination mints the
// element of ITS container BEFORE the call and leaves it unfinished across the call (the twin
// builds the moved field in it), finishing it only where the plain form moved.  So in one
// loop two appends interleave: an element of `items` minted ahead and possibly released, and
// a plain record appended to `trail` around it — the bare append must take its own slot in
// its own vector whatever the other element's state, and the failing path's release must
// leave `trail`'s elements alone.
//
// Axes it MOVES: the destination call's outcome (ok, fails mid-list), the other append's
// position (before the call inside the window's block, after the loop), the vectors' store
// (separate vectors, two fields of one record — the field clause), growth (a list longer than
// the first claim).  The format, one byte a head: below 100 an integer, 200 + n
// a list of n values.  Values hand-computed in each cell's comment.
//
// Firing read under `LOFT_TRACE_DESTINATION=1` ("n_walk builds 1 call(s) of n_dv(…) in one
// element") and off `--native-emit`: the destination's element minted ahead of the call by the
// bare clause's `vector_record_append` and finished on the ok path, `trail`'s appends through
// a group header, d4's field appends as `field_vector_append`.  Native only; the interpreter is the oracle.  `LOFT_NO_DESTINATION=1`,
// `LOFT_NO_BARE_APPEND=1` and `LOFT_NO_FIELD_APPEND=1` answer the same, and so do
// `LOFT_POISON=1` and `LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1`.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; a bare append that took the destination's minted
//   element as its slot would answer d2's trail with the item's bytes.
// CHANNEL: the asserts.
// ARMED: LOFT_POISON=1, LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1.
// WITNESS: each message prints the item sum and the trail.
// HOLDS: the interpreter is the oracle.

struct Pt { x: integer, y: integer = 7, tag: text, z: integer? }
struct Bag { pad: integer, pts: vector<Pt>, ints: vector<integer>, chars: vector<character> }
enum Sh { Rect { w: integer, h: integer, tag: integer }, Circle { r: integer }, Dot }
struct Scene { n: integer, shapes: vector<Sh> }
struct Item { id: integer, v: integer }
struct Entry { id: integer, v: integer }
struct Sorted { by_id: sorted<Item[id]> }
struct Hashed { table: hash<Entry[id]> }

fn grow(b: Bag, n: integer) {
  if n == 0 { return; }
  b.pts += [Pt { x: n }];
  b.ints += [n * n];
  b.chars += [if n % 2 == 0 { 'é' } else { 'z' }];
  grow(b, n - 1);
}

fn add(s: Scene, k: integer) {
  s.shapes += [Rect { w: k, h: k + 1, tag: 9 }];
  s.shapes += [Circle { r: k }];
  s.shapes += [Dot];
}

fn describe(sh: Sh) -> text {
  match sh {
    Rect { w, h, tag } => "R{w}x{h}/{tag}",
    Circle { r } => "C{r}",
    Dot => "D",
  }
}

fn mk(k: integer) -> Pt { Pt { x: k, y: k * 2, tag: "t{k}" } }

fn sums(v: vector<Pt>) -> text {
  sx = 0;
  sy = 0;
  tags = "";
  for p in v { sx += p.x; sy += p.y; tags += p.tag; }
  "{len(v)}:{sx}:{sy}:{tags}"
}

// the bare clause: two vectors appended in one loop, one of them from a call's result, the
// other from a literal beside a conditional second append
fn two(n: integer) -> text {
  a: vector<Pt> = [];
  b: vector<Pt> = [];
  for i in 0..n {
    a += [Pt { x: i }];
    b += [mk(i)];
    if i % 2 == 0 { a += [mk(i + 100)]; }
  }
  "{sums(a)} | {sums(b)}"
}

// a vector parameter appended in a loop that calls a writer in between
fn dyn(n: integer, v: vector<Pt>) -> text {
  for i in 0..n {
    t = mk(i);
    v += [Pt { x: t.x, tag: t.tag }];
  }
  sums(v)
}

// a `while` with a `break` once the vector holds more than 100 records (growth past several steps)
fn whiler(n: integer) -> text {
  out: vector<Pt> = [];
  i = 0;
  while i < n {
    j = mk(i);
    out += [Pt { x: i, tag: j.tag }];
    i += 1;
    if len(out) > 100 { break; }
  }
  sums(out)
}

fn tags_to(n: integer) -> text {
  out = "";
  for i in 0..n { out += "t{i}"; }
  out
}

fn test_a_straight_line_append_to_a_record_field_is_its_own_slot_for_every_ele() {
  b = Bag { pad: 3, pts: [], ints: [], chars: [] };
  grow(b, 30);
  assert(len(b.pts) == 30 && len(b.ints) == 30 && len(b.chars) == 30 && b.pad == 3, "lengths {len(b.pts)} {len(b.ints)} {len(b.chars)}");
  sx = 0;
  sy = 0;
  empties = 0;
  nulls = 0;
  for p in b.pts {
    sx += p.x;
    sy += p.y;
    if p.tag == "" { empties += 1; }
    if p.z == null { nulls += 1; }
  }
  assert(sx == 465 && sy == 210 && empties == 30 && nulls == 30, "pts {sx} {sy} {empties} {nulls}");
  assert(b.pts[0].x == 30 && b.pts[14].x == 16 && b.pts[29].x == 1, "pts order {b.pts[0].x} {b.pts[14].x} {b.pts[29].x}");
  si = 0;
  for q in b.ints { si += q; }
  assert(si == 9455 && b.ints[0] == 900 && b.ints[29] == 1, "ints {si} {b.ints[0]} {b.ints[29]}");
  acute = 0;
  zed = 0;
  for c in b.chars { if c == 'é' { acute += 1; } else if c == 'z' { zed += 1; } }
  assert(acute == 15 && zed == 15 && b.chars[0] == 'é' && b.chars[29] == 'z', "chars {acute} {zed}");
  b0 = Bag { pad: 0, pts: [], ints: [], chars: [] };
  grow(b0, 0);
  assert(len(b0.pts) == 0 && len(b0.ints) == 0, "zero appends {len(b0.pts)}");
  b1 = Bag { pad: 0, pts: [], ints: [], chars: [] };
  grow(b1, 1);
  assert(len(b1.pts) == 1 && b1.pts[0].x == 1 && b1.pts[0].y == 7 && b1.pts[0].tag == "" && b1.pts[0].z == null, "one append {len(b1.pts)}");

  s = Scene { n: 0, shapes: [] };
  add(s, 1);
  add(s, 2);
  assert(len(s.shapes) == 6, "shapes {len(s.shapes)}");
  area = 0;
  rad = 0;
  tags = 0;
  dots = 0;
  for sh in s.shapes {
    match sh {
      Rect { w, h, tag } => { area += w * h; tags += tag; },
      Circle { r } => { rad += r; },
      Dot => { dots += 1; },
    }
  }
  assert(area == 8 && rad == 3 && tags == 18 && dots == 2, "enum totals {area} {rad} {tags} {dots}");
  assert(describe(s.shapes[0]) == "R1x2/9" && describe(s.shapes[1]) == "C1" && describe(s.shapes[2]) == "D", "positions 0-2 {describe(s.shapes[0])} {describe(s.shapes[1])} {describe(s.shapes[2])}");
  assert(describe(s.shapes[3]) == "R2x3/9" && describe(s.shapes[4]) == "C2" && describe(s.shapes[5]) == "D", "positions 3-5 {describe(s.shapes[3])} {describe(s.shapes[4])} {describe(s.shapes[5])}");

  kd = Sorted { by_id: [] };
  kh = Hashed { table: [] };
  kd.by_id += [Item { id: 5, v: 50 }];
  kd.by_id += [Item { id: 3, v: 30 }];
  kd.by_id += [Item { id: 9, v: 90 }];
  kh.table += [Entry { id: 5, v: 50 }];
  kh.table += [Entry { id: 3, v: 30 }];
  kh.table += [Entry { id: 9, v: 90 }];
  order = "";
  for it in kd.by_id { order += "{it.id}"; }
  assert(order == "359", "sorted field order {order}");
  assert((kh.table[9].v ?? -1) == 90 && (kh.table[3].v ?? -1) == 30 && (kh.table[4].v ?? -1) == -1 && len(kh.table) == 3, "hash field lookups");

  t2 = two(5);
  assert(t2 == "8:316:647:t100t102t104 | 5:10:20:t0t1t2t3t4", "two {t2}");
  v0: vector<Pt> = [];
  d3 = dyn(3, v0);
  assert(d3 == "3:3:21:t0t1t2", "dyn {d3}");
  v1: vector<Pt> = [];
  assert(dyn(0, v1) == "0:0:0:", "dyn zero");
  w4 = whiler(4);
  assert(w4 == "4:6:28:t0t1t2t3", "while {w4}");
  w200 = whiler(200);
  assert(w200 == "101:5050:707:" + tags_to(101), "while with a break {w200}");
}

enum Val { VNone, VInt { value: integer }, VList { items: vector<Val> } }
struct Dv { const value: Val, const next: integer, const ok: boolean }
struct Mark { at: integer, ok: boolean }
struct Log { pad: integer, marks: vector<Mark>, firsts: vector<Mark> }

fn dv(b: vector<u8>, pos: integer) -> Dv {
  if pos >= len(b) { return Dv { value: VNone, next: pos, ok: false }; }
  t = b[pos] ?? 0;
  if t < 100 { return Dv { value: VInt { value: t }, next: pos + 1, ok: true }; }
  if t < 200 { return Dv { value: VNone, next: pos, ok: false }; }
  items: vector<Val> = [];
  p = pos + 1;
  aok = true;
  for _ in 0..t - 200 {
    if aok {
      sub = dv(b, p);
      if sub.ok { items += [sub.value]; p = sub.next; } else { aok = false; }
    }
  }
  if !aok { return Dv { value: VNone, next: pos, ok: false }; }
  return Dv { value: VList { items: items }, next: p, ok: true };
}

fn ints(v: Val) -> integer {
  match v {
    VInt { value } => value,
    VList { items } => { s = 0; for x in items { s += ints(x); } s },
    _ => 0
  }
}

/// A top-level list walked by hand: each element decoded by the destination twin into `items`,
/// a Mark appended to `trail` before every call and once after the loop.  The `if aok` block
/// with nothing after the result's `if` is the shape the destination site admits in this build
/// (the call at the loop body's own top level, an append inside the `ok` arm or a statement
/// after the `if` keep the plain move — R-Place's loop clause then places the result).
fn walk(b: vector<u8>, trail: vector<Mark>) -> integer {
  items: vector<Val> = [];
  p = 1;
  aok = true;
  n = (b[0] ?? 200) - 200;
  for _ in 0..n {
    if aok {
      trail += [Mark { at: p, ok: false }];
      sub = dv(b, p);
      if sub.ok {
        items += [sub.value];
        p = sub.next;
      } else {
        aok = false;
      }
    }
  }
  trail += [Mark { at: p, ok: aok }];
  s = 0;
  for x in items { s += ints(x); }
  s * 100 + len(items)
}

fn trail_sum(t: const vector<Mark>) -> integer { s = 0; for m in t { s += m.at * (if m.ok { 2 } else { 1 }); } s }

// d1 — [5, [7, 8], 9]: three calls, all ok.  items Σ 29, len 3 → 2903.  trail: (1,f) (2,f)
// (5,f) and after the loop (6,t) → Σ at·(ok ? 2 : 1) = 1 + 2 + 5 + 12 = 20, len 4.
fn test_every_call_ok() {
  b: vector<u8> = [203, 5, 202, 7, 8, 9];
  trail: vector<Mark> = [];
  r = walk(b, trail);
  assert(r == 2903 && len(trail) == 4 && trail_sum(trail) == 20, "d1 {r} {trail_sum(trail)}");
}

// d2 — [4, [6, <end>]]: the second call fails (its inner list runs out): items [4], Σ 4 →
// 401; trail (1,f) (2,f) and after the loop (2,f) → 5, len 3.
fn test_a_failing_call_releases_its_element_and_keeps_the_trail() {
  b: vector<u8> = [202, 4, 202, 6];
  trail: vector<Mark> = [];
  r = walk(b, trail);
  assert(r == 401 && len(trail) == 3 && trail_sum(trail) == 5 && !trail[2].ok, "d2 {r} {trail_sum(trail)}");
}

// d3 — a list of 15 integers 1..15 (past the first claim of both vectors): items Σ 120 →
// 12015; trail 16 marks: 1 + … + 15 = 120 before the calls, (16,t) after → 152.
fn test_both_vectors_past_their_first_claim() {
  b: vector<u8> = [215];
  for i in 1..16 { b += [(i & 255) as u8]; }
  trail: vector<Mark> = [];
  r = walk(b, trail);
  assert(r == 12015 && len(trail) == 16 && trail_sum(trail) == 152, "d3 {r} {trail_sum(trail)}");
}

// d4 — the FIELD clause after the loop: two plain-vector fields of one record appended
// straight-line, then a destination walk in between: marks [(1,t)], walk of [9] → 901,
// firsts [(9,f)], marks += [(2,f)] → marks Σ 2 + 2 = 4, firsts Σ 9.
fn test_field_appends_around_a_walk() {
  lg = Log { pad: 0, marks: [], firsts: [] };
  lg.marks += [Mark { at: 1, ok: true }];
  b: vector<u8> = [201, 9];
  tr: vector<Mark> = [];
  r = walk(b, tr);
  lg.firsts += [Mark { at: r / 100, ok: false }];
  lg.marks += [Mark { at: 2, ok: false }];
  assert(r == 901 && len(lg.marks) == 2 && trail_sum(lg.marks) == 4 && trail_sum(lg.firsts) == 9 && len(tr) == 2, "d4 {r}");
}

